  "type": "Checkpoint",
  "number": "2.5.11",
  "title": "Why only in three dimensions?",
  "body": " Why only in three dimensions?   The dot product makes sense for vectors in any dimension, yet the cross product lives only in three. Explain why a plane in can hand its oriented area to a single normal vector, while the same trick fails both in and in .    Count the directions perpendicular to a plane. In there are none to spare; in there are too many to choose from. Only one dimension leaves exactly one perpendicular line.   "
