  "body": " Zero determinant and collapse in the plane   For a matrix , the following statements are equivalent:   .  the columns of are linearly dependent.  collapses the plane onto a line or a point.  is not invertible.     The determinant of is the signed area of the parallelogram spanned by its columns. That area is zero exactly when the two columns lie on one line, which means they are linearly dependent.  If the columns lie on one line, then every output lies on that same line. So the plane collapses onto a line, or onto a point if both columns are zero.  A collapse cannot be inverted, because many different input vectors land on the same output. Conversely, if the columns are independent, they form a basis of the plane, so every output has exactly one coordinate description in those columns. Then is invertible.   "
