  "body": " How can yet the flux be ?   For the inverse-square field, everywhere the form is defined, and yet the outward flux through a sphere around the origin is , not . What is it about the hole at the origin that lets a source hide there, even though the source density vanishes at every point you can actually evaluate?    The divergence theorem trades a boundary integral for an interior integral only when the form is defined on the whole solid. Here the origin is missing, so the interior integral is not allowed to swallow that point. Ask where the theorem's hypothesis quietly fails.   "
