  "body": " Eigenvalues and eigenvectors   Definition  Let be an matrix.  A nonzero vector is an eigenvector of if for some scalar .  The scalar is the eigenvalue.    Meaning  An eigenvector is a direction that does not turn.  The matrix only stretches, shrinks, or reverses that direction.       Finding eigenvalues  Start with Rewrite: For a nonzero solution , the matrix must be singular:   This equation is the characteristic equation.    Characteristic polynomial     Eigenvalues are the roots of     shortcut  If then and   The characteristic equation can be written as     Finding eigenvectors  For each eigenvalue , solve   The solution space is the eigenspace for .     Remember: The zero vector is never called an eigenvector.    Diagonal matrices  If then the standard basis vectors are eigenvectors:     Diagonalization  If has linearly independent eigenvectors then put them into a matrix   Let   Then     Powers  If then   For a diagonal matrix,     Symmetric matrices  If then:   Thus a symmetric matrix can be written as where is orthogonal:   Symmetric matrices are the clean case. Hessian matrices are symmetric when mixed partials agree.    Common warnings   Eigenvectors must be nonzero.  Eigenvalues can be repeated.  A matrix may fail to have enough eigenvectors to diagonalize.  Eigenvalues are defined only for square matrices.  For real matrices, complex eigenvalues may occur unless the matrix is symmetric.    "
